a game designed for the Nintendo DS which is a clone of Warcraft Rumble.

The game is designed for a vertical orientation device, so the DS will suffice.

the intended gameplay architecture is an Anbernic DS device. That's it's name.

Here is a limited description of the mechanics:

gameplay takes place on a map. This map is two-tiles-by-two-tiles, a departure
from Warcraft Rumble. In Symbeline Rumble, pushing R will move you to the right
map (from the left map) and pushing it again will open up a menu. From the
right map, pushing L will bring you to the left map, and pushing it again will
open the same menu. Specifically, the menu to do operations related to the units
on that part of the map - you can cast spells this way, rather than managing
them on the touchscreen. The Anbernic DS has the capability to tap on the top,
but the original hardware does not. Depending on how the scope is delineated,
we may or may not be able to target above directly. If not, then we must do it
(cast spells) by casting them on a unit. (Or, indeed, perhaps not at all)

In this map, the enemy typically has a home base or territory, and the player
does as well. Units will path outward from the places where they are spawned
(next to a barracks or guardhouse) and follow the natural curves of the map.
The paths are pre-defined, just as in a strategy game they are coralled. Think
WC3 style maps, with pathways that go about. Through trees and such, WC3 maps
are renowned for being detailed in their architecture. Such a powerful engine.

When they encounter combat, they do battle. It is valorous on both sides.

Units are recruited with gold that is earned passively and actively, from
gemstone mines and treasure-chests. Units must bring back treasure for it to be
counted. Then they return to the fight.

other mechanics are similar to Warcraft Rumble. The game will be 3D, with a
tilt-shift inspired visual dynamic. No units will be on parts of the map that
are blurred, only scenery.

there are no bars on the map, nor menus, nor text. Some button prompts or
notifications may be shown on the map, speech-bubble style.

gold is shown as section-marks on a bar near important infrastructure - the
total gold that may be accumulated is determined by the structures you claim on
the map. If units are unplayable, they are disabled and removed from your
cycling unit deck until they are playable again.

units in this game are adventurers that gain equipment, classes, and levels
inbetween matches. Sorta like Fire Emblem. They behave different on the map,
according to how you spec them, and also the behavior patterns you request be
applied.

this is the basic vision, other things should be considered as they come up.
